# aiohttp is transitive (elasticsearch / google-* / locust / anthropic) — pin
# the security floor explicitly. CVE-2026-34993 (CookieJar.load code-exec on
# untrusted input) + CVE-2026-47265 (cookies leak across cross-origin
# redirects), both fixed in 3.14.0. CVE-2026-54280 (payload resource not
# closed on mid-write client disconnect → resource starvation), fixed in
# 3.14.1 — bumped 2026-06-15 after pip-audit started flagging it (broke
# dev CI's Audit-Python-dependencies step across unrelated commits).
# Three more, all fixed by 3.14.3 — bumped 2026-08-04, same Audit-step
# breakage: CVE-2026-59881 (client decompresses RSV1 frames even when
# permessage-deflate was not negotiated), CVE-2026-69243 (request smuggling
# via an edge case in the WebSocket upgrade), CVE-2026-69244 (out-of-bounds
# heap read in the C response parser while building a malformed-response
# error). We use aiohttp transitively as an HTTP client, not a server, so
# the smuggling issue does not apply; the parser read is client-side and is
# the one that mattered here.
aiohttp>=3.14.3
